---
// Ported from ~/Git/12f.dk/content/rankmaster/privacy-policy.md, written
// 2026-09-16. The substance is not rewritten here — changing what this page
// says is a legal decision, not a design one (CLAUDE.md §6). Only the markup
// and the internal links have changed, and the "Last Updated: see top of page"
// placeholder has become a real date.
//
// The declarations here must not claim less collection than the App Store
// nutrition label in fastlane/privacy.json: coarse location, email, name,
// product interaction and user ID — analytics and app functionality, all
// linked to you, none used for tracking.
import ProsePage from "../layouts/ProsePage.astro";
import { SITE, SUPPORT_EMAIL, ORG_NAME, ORG_CVR, ORG_URL, APP_SHORT_NAME } from "../consts";

const UPDATED = "2026-09-16";
const title = "Privacy policy — RankMaster";
const description =
  "What RankMaster stores, what appears publicly on the leaderboards, what the analytics collect, and how to delete everything. Written to meet our obligations under the GDPR.";
---

<ProsePage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={`${SITE}/privacy-policy.html`}
  heading="Privacy policy"
  standfirst={`How ${APP_SHORT_NAME} handles information when you use our iOS application.`}
  updated={UPDATED}
>
  <p>
    This privacy policy describes how {APP_SHORT_NAME} handles information when you use our iOS
    application. {APP_SHORT_NAME} is published by {ORG_NAME}, a Danish company (CVR {ORG_CVR}), and
    this policy is written to meet our obligations under the EU General Data Protection Regulation
    (GDPR).
  </p>

  <h2>In short</h2>
  <div class="summary">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>
          {APP_SHORT_NAME} has a server, and this is the one 12F app where that matters.
        </strong>
        Most of our apps keep everything on your device. This one has leaderboards, and a leaderboard
        only exists if scores leave the device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your display name is public.</strong> It is shown to other players on the
        leaderboards. That is the point of it. Nothing else about you is shown to anyone.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can play <strong>without an account</strong>, and if you do, nothing you play is sent to
        us at all.
      </li>
      <li>
        We collect <strong>anonymous usage analytics</strong> so we can see which parts of the game
        work. There is currently <strong>no in-app switch to turn this off</strong> — see below,
        where we say so plainly rather than implying otherwise.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>We do not track you.</strong> We never access the advertising identifier (IDFA), we
        show no ads, we use no advertising or attribution software, and we never share your data with
        data brokers or combine it with data from other companies.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Playing without an account</h2>
  <p>
    When you first open {APP_SHORT_NAME} you are given an anonymous session so that you can start
    playing immediately — there is no sign-up wall. That session is a random identifier belonging to
    one installation of the app on one device.
  </p>
  <p>
    While you are anonymous, <strong>rounds you play are not sent to our server</strong>. No score,
    no run history, no leaderboard entry. What the app remembers — which questions you have seen,
    whether you have played today&rsquo;s daily challenge, whether you have finished the introduction
    — is stored on your device only.
  </p>

  <h2>If you sign in with Apple</h2>
  <p>
    Signing in is optional and exists for one reason: so that your record survives a new phone and
    can appear on a leaderboard.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What Apple tells us.</strong> We use Sign in with Apple. Apple sends us a token
    identifying you and, on the <strong>first</strong> authorisation only, the name and email address
    you choose to share. You may use Apple&rsquo;s <em>Hide My Email</em> relay, in which case we
    never see your real address. We use the name only to offer it to you as a display name; we do not
    email you.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What we then store on our server</strong>, which is operated by us in the EU:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>An account identifier, and the Apple identity linked to it</li>
    <li>
      A profile: your <strong>display name</strong>, a country code derived from your device&rsquo;s
      region, and a seed used to draw your avatar
    </li>
    <li>
      A run log: for each recorded round, the score, how many questions it had, how many you got
      exactly right, your best run, the mode and category, your language, the app version and when
      you played
    </li>
    <li>One best-score row per leaderboard you appear on</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>What is public.</strong> Your <strong>display name</strong> and your score are shown to
    other players on the leaderboards. Your country code is stored but is not displayed. Your email
    address, your Apple Account and your account identifier are never shown to anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    You choose the display name. You are given a generated one such as <em>Quiet Meridian</em> when
    you start, and you can change it at any time under the profile icon → <strong>Change name</strong
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    We collect anonymous usage data to understand how people play {APP_SHORT_NAME} and where they get
    stuck. This is done with <strong>PostHog</strong>, hosted in the EU —
    <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>What we collect</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Which mode you chose and whether you arrived from a link</li>
    <li>
      That a round started, finished or could not be dealt, with the score, the number of questions,
      how many were exact and your best run
    </li>
    <li>Whether a score saved, queued, failed or was skipped</li>
    <li>
      That an account was created, upgraded, signed out or deleted; that a name change was accepted
      or rejected; that a sign-in offer was shown, opened or dismissed
    </li>
    <li>Which leaderboard was viewed, and that a review prompt was asked for</li>
    <li>
      Technical context added by the analytics software: device model, iOS version, app version,
      language and region, and whether the build is from the App Store, TestFlight, a simulator or a
      development machine
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>What we do not collect</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your display name, your country code or your email address</li>
    <li>The questions you were asked or the answers you gave</li>
    <li>Precise location</li>
    <li>The advertising identifier (IDFA)</li>
    <li>Any screen recording or screenshot of the app</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is enforced by how the app is built, not only by policy: the analytics interface has no
    free-form field, so a new piece of information cannot be sent without a deliberate code change.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Identifiers.</strong> So that we can count distinct players rather than distinct sessions,
    analytics events carry the account identifier described above. It is meaningless outside {
      APP_SHORT_NAME
    } and cannot be used to track you across other companies&rsquo; apps or websites.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Approximate location.</strong> As with any internet request, your IP address reaches our
    analytics provider. PostHog derives an approximate country and city from it and attaches that to
    the event. The app neither sends nor can switch this off — it is a setting on our analytics
    account — but because it is collected against your account identifier we declare it as coarse
    location in the app&rsquo;s privacy report on the App Store.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Turning analytics off.</strong> There is currently no setting in the app to switch
    analytics off. We would rather say that here than leave you looking for one. If you would like
    your analytics data removed, write to us and we will delete the records held against your account
    identifier.
  </p>

  <h2>Tracking</h2>
  <p>
    {APP_SHORT_NAME} does not track you, as the term is used in Apple&rsquo;s App Tracking
    Transparency framework and in Apple&rsquo;s App Store guidelines. Specifically:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      We do not access the advertising identifier (IDFA), and the app contains no advertising,
      attribution or ad-network software.
    </li>
    <li>We do not link the data we collect with data from other companies for advertising purposes.</li>
    <li>We never share your data with data brokers.</li>
    <li>The app shows no advertising.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For this reason the app does not ask for App Tracking Transparency permission — there is nothing
    to authorise.
  </p>

  <h2>Permissions the app asks for</h2>
  <p>
    {APP_SHORT_NAME} asks for no device permissions. It does not use your camera, photos, contacts,
    microphone, calendar, notifications or location services.
  </p>

  <h2>Where your data is held</h2>
  <p>
    Accounts, profiles, scores and leaderboards are stored on servers operated by {ORG_NAME} in the
    EU. Analytics are held by PostHog in the EU. We do not sell data, and we do not transfer it
    outside the EU/EEA for these purposes.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your account, profile and leaderboard entries are kept until you delete your account.</li>
    <li>
      Daily leaderboards are removed after 30 days and weekly leaderboards after 12 weeks,
      automatically.
    </li>
    <li>
      Analytics are kept only as long as they are useful for improving the game, subject to
      PostHog&rsquo;s own retention policy.
    </li>
    <li>Anything stored only on your device goes when you delete the app.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Children&rsquo;s privacy</h2>
  <p>
    {APP_SHORT_NAME} is a general-audience game and is not directed at children. We do not knowingly
    collect personal information from children. Because display names are public, please choose one
    that does not identify you.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR you have the right to access, correct, delete, restrict or object to the
    processing of your personal data, and the right to data portability and to lodge a complaint with
    a supervisory authority (in Denmark, Datatilsynet).
  </p>
  <p>Two of these you can exercise in the app immediately:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Correct</strong> — change your display name at any time under the profile icon →
      <strong>Change name</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Delete</strong> — profile icon → <strong>Delete account</strong> removes your account,
      your profile and your leaderboard entries from our server. Deleting the app alone does not do
      this; it removes what is on the device and leaves the account behind.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For anything else, including a copy of the data held against your account, write to us at the
    address below.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this privacy policy</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this privacy policy from time to time. We will post the new version here and update
    the date at the top of the page.
  </p>

  <div class="prose__contact">
    <h2>Contact us</h2>
    <p>
      If you have any questions about this privacy policy, or want to exercise any of the rights
      above, please contact us:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Email: <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a></li>
      <li>{ORG_NAME}, CVR {ORG_CVR}, Denmark</li>
      <li>Website: <a href={ORG_URL} rel="noopener">12f.dk</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</ProsePage>
